  "notes": "West leads the ♠K against 5♣. West's spade lead wins the first trick and he switches to a trump. South has 3 heart losers to deal with. At first glance it appears that trumps will need to be 2-2.\n\nIn this case, South can draw a second round of trump, unblock the diamonds, and surrender a heart trick. One of South's heart losers can be ruffed in the dummy. Dummy's diamonds will provide a discard for South's remaining heart.\n\nBy proper play, South can also succeed when trumps are 3-1 as long as the defender with a singleton club also has the ♥A. South should lead the ♥K at trick 3. On the given lie of the cards, East wins the ♥A, but he has no more trumps to play. If East had a trump to return, South would need a 2-2 trump break.\n\nEast will return a diamond, won in the closed hand. Dummy still has 2 trumps left to take care of South's losing hearts. South should make sure to use spade ruffs as hand entries. To play diamonds would risk a defensive ruff.",
  "commentary_paragraphs": [
